# Project Charter — Initiating
#
# Field list follows Process Groups: A Practice Guide, process 4.1 Develop Project Charter.
# Origin "yours" marks a field that is not in the standard.
#
# This file is the study guide. Edit the wording here and the app changes with
# no code change.

screen: charter
title: Project Charter
phase: Initiating
process: 4.1 Develop Project Charter
order: 10
teaches: |
  Why a project has to be authorized before anyone works on it, and what a
  sponsor is actually signing. By the end you should be able to say what a
  charter contains, who signs it, and why starting the plan before it is signed
  is the most common way a project goes wrong early.
before: |
  Nothing. The charter is where a project starts. If work has already been
  going on for weeks, that is normal, and writing the charter now is how you
  find out what everyone has been assuming.
after: |
  Everything downstream. The charter's stakeholder list becomes the
  stakeholder register; its high-level requirements become the requirements
  screen; its milestones become the schedule; its boundaries become the scope
  statement's exclusions.
intro: |
  The charter is the document that formally authorizes a project to exist. It
  names the project, says why it exists, draws its boundaries, and gives the
  project manager authority to spend resources on it.

  Nothing downstream — requirements, schedule, risks — should be started until
  the sponsor signs it.
source: |
  Process Groups: A Practice Guide (PMI, 2022), process 4.1 Develop Project
  Charter, in the Initiating process group. The field list below follows that
  process's charter contents.

  That guide carries forward the 49 processes and their artifact contents from
  the PMBOK Guide 6th edition, which PMI retired in 2022. The 7th edition
  replaced the processes with principles; the 8th, published in 2025,
  reorganizes the same ground into focus areas and performance domains. Neither
  carries the prescriptive content lists these fields come from, which is why
  the practice guide is cited here. The process numbering is the practice
  guide's.

field purpose
  prompt: Why does this project exist, and what will it produce?
  check words>=10: A purpose in under ten words is a title. Say what it produces and why that matters, because this is the sentence you will hold every later suggestion up against.
  label: Project title and purpose
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: Charter content: project purpose.
  why: |
    It is the "why are we doing this" that every later choice gets checked
    against. When someone proposes a feature, this is the sentence you hold it
    up against.
  if_blank: |
    Scope drifts, because nothing can be ruled out. Every suggestion sounds
    reasonable when there is no stated purpose to contradict it.
  example: |
    Build a PM product that works as a study guide, practical application and
    future organization.

field business_case
  prompt: Why is this worth the time compared with everything else you could be doing?
  check words>=12: Too short to defend. A business case has to beat the alternatives, including doing nothing at all.
  label: Business case / justification
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Business documents (the business case and benefits management plan), which
    are inputs to 4.1 and feed the charter.
  why: |
    It answers "why is this worth the time and money compared to anything
    else." The purpose says what the project is; the business case says why it
    beats the alternatives, including doing nothing.
  if_blank: |
    The project has no defense when priorities shift. Sponsors cancel projects
    that cannot say why they matter.
  example: |
    No existing tool holds project-level truth: charter, scope, decisions,
    risks and status against a plan.

field success_criteria
  prompt: How will you know it worked? Write conditions you could check as true or false.
  check words>=12: Thin for success criteria. Each one should be something a person could check without arguing about it.
  label: Measurable objectives and success criteria
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: Charter content: measurable project objectives and related success criteria.
  why: |
    It is how you will know the project worked, which is a different question
    from whether it shipped. "Measurable" is the whole point: each criterion
    should be something you can check as true or false without arguing.
  if_blank: |
    The project cannot succeed or fail. It just stops, and nobody can say
    whether it was worth doing.
  example: |
    Every field in the tool has a source tag and a "why" explanation.
    This project runs start to finish inside the tool.

field high_level_requirements
  prompt: At this level, what does it have to do? Just enough to size the work.
  check filled: Without this, nobody can tell whether the project is sized right until the build is underway.
  label: High-level requirements
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter content: high-level requirements. Detailed requirements come later,
    in Planning (5.2 Collect Requirements).
  why: |
    Just enough to size the work and catch a wrong direction early.
  if_blank: |
    Nobody can tell whether the project is sized right until the build is
    already underway. Too much detail here is also a failure: it is Planning
    work done before the project is authorized.
  example: |
    Guided input forms for each lifecycle artifact.
    Built-in explanation for each field.

field description_deliverables
  prompt: What will actually be handed over when this is done?
  check words>=8: Name the thing that will exist at the end. A deliverable nobody can picture gets built as whatever seemed obvious.
  label: High-level description and key deliverables
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: Charter content: high-level project description, boundaries and key deliverables.
  why: |
    It says what the project will actually hand over when it is done.
  if_blank: |
    The team builds whatever seems obvious, and each person's version of
    obvious is different.
  example: |
    Personal, installable desktop software covering the full project lifecycle.

field out_of_scope
  prompt: What will this project deliberately NOT do?
  check words>=6: An empty exclusions list is how scope creep starts. Name at least one thing you are refusing to do.
  label: Boundaries: out of scope
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter content: project boundaries. Formalized later in the scope
    statement's exclusions (5.3 Define Scope).
  why: |
    Writing down what the project will NOT do prevents more scope creep than
    any in-scope list does. An in-scope list grows; an out-of-scope list is
    something you can point at.
  if_blank: |
    Every good idea becomes an implied requirement, and the project never
    finishes.
  example: |
    Multi-user permissions. Gantt and resource-leveling math.

field overall_risk
  prompt: Overall, how risky is this, and what worries you most?
  check filled: Without a stated risk posture, bad news later arrives as a surprise instead of as something you were watching.
  label: Overall project risk
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter content: overall project risk. The full risk register comes in
    Planning (11.2 Identify Risks).
  why: |
    It sets the project's risk posture at the start, so bad news later is not a
    surprise.
  if_blank: |
    Risks are discovered as problems instead of being watched as possibilities.
  example: |
    A single late review gate: defects found at the most expensive moment.

field milestones
  prompt: What are the dated checkpoints between now and done?
  check rows>=3: Fewer than three checkpoints is a start date and a hope. Milestones are what turn 'in progress' into something that can be late.
  check column:target: A milestone with no date is not a milestone. Give every row a target.
  label: Summary milestone schedule
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: timeline
  start_column: target
  finish_column: target
  title_column: milestone
  tone_column: status
  source: |
    Charter content: summary milestone schedule. The detailed schedule comes in
    Planning (6.5 Develop Schedule).
  why: |
    Milestones are dated checkpoints, not tasks. They are what turn "in
    progress" into something that can be on time or late.
  if_blank: |
    "In progress" never ends, and nobody notices a slip until it is large.
  example: |
    M1 Charter approved | 2026-09-15 | Reached | "hopefully today"
  column milestone
    label: Milestone
    type: line
    width: 4
  column target
    label: Target date
    type: date
    width: 2
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Not started=faint, In progress=accent, Reached=ok, Missed=danger, Rebaselined=warn
    width: 2
  column notes
    label: Notes
    type: line
    width: 4

field budget
  prompt: How much can be spent before you have to go back to the sponsor?
  check filled: Without a cap, either every purchase needs a conversation or spending grows unchecked. Zero is a valid answer; write it down.
  label: Pre-approved financial resources
  type: line
  origin: standard
  source: Charter content: preapproved financial resources.
  why: |
    It sets how much can be spent before anyone has to go back to the sponsor.
  if_blank: |
    Either every purchase needs a conversation, or spending grows unchecked.
  example: $0 beyond existing subscriptions and hosting.

field key_stakeholders
  prompt: Who can affect this project, or be affected by it?
  check filled: The stakeholder you forget is the one who turns up late with an objection nobody planned for.
  label: Key stakeholder list
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter content: key stakeholder list. Expanded in the Stakeholder Register
    (13.1 Identify Stakeholders).
  why: |
    Everyone who is affected by the project, or can affect it, gets named up
    front.
  if_blank: |
    An unlisted stakeholder shows up late with an objection nobody planned for.
  example: |
    Sponsor / PM / primary user; final reviewer; delivery team. Details on the
    Stakeholder Register screen.

field approval_requirements
  prompt: What does 'done and accepted' mean, and who decides?
  check words>=10: Say who signs off and against what. 'Done' argued about at the end is the most expensive argument on a project.
  label: Project approval requirements
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter content: project approval requirements — what constitutes success,
    who decides, and who signs off.
  why: |
    It defines what "done and accepted" means, and who has the authority to say
    so.
  if_blank: |
    "Done" gets argued about at the end, when it is most expensive to disagree.
  example: |
    You accept each milestone. The final reviewer gives the verdict on whether
    it is right.

field exit_criteria
  prompt: What would make you close this project, and what would make you cancel it?
  check mentions:cancel,stop,abandon,close early,kill: Only the closing half is here. Say what would make you cancel it too: most personal projects never end on purpose, they just fade out.
  label: Project exit criteria
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter content: project exit criteria — the conditions to close or cancel
    the project or phase.
  why: |
    It sets the conditions for closing the project, or for cancelling it on
    purpose. Cancelling on purpose is a legitimate outcome; fading out is not.
  if_blank: |
    The project never ends on purpose. It just fades out, and nothing is
    learned from it.
  example: |
    Cancel if no screen is used on a real project for 30 days.

field pm_authority
  prompt: What can the project manager decide without asking anyone?
  check filled: Without this, every decision becomes a negotiation, or someone makes a call they did not have the authority to make.
  label: Assigned project manager, responsibility and authority
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: Charter content: assigned project manager, responsibility and authority level.
  why: |
    It says who can make which calls without asking.
  if_blank: |
    Every decision becomes a negotiation, or someone makes a call they did not
    have the authority to make.
  example: |
    PM: you. The delivery team implements but does not decide.

field sponsor
  prompt: Who is sponsoring this, and what authority do they have?
  check filled: Without a named sponsor, nobody has the standing to approve, defend or end the project.
  label: Sponsor and authority
  type: line
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Charter content: name and authority of the sponsor or other person(s)
    authorizing the charter.
  why: |
    The sponsor funds the project, protects it, and signs the charter.
  if_blank: |
    Nobody has the standing to approve, defend or end the project.
  example: You.

field decision_rule
  prompt: How will you record where a decision came from?
  check filled: Without a rule, 'we decided X' gets said about things nobody decided.
  label: Decision provenance rule
  type: text
  origin: yours
  source: |
    Not in the standard. Your rule, from 2026-08-27: a decision is only
    attributed to you with a quote and a date.
  why: |
    A decision record is only trustworthy if it shows where each decision came
    from. Three states: specified (you said it), agreed (you approved a
    summary), unobjected (proposed, no reply). Only the first two are
    decisions.
  if_blank: |
    "You decided X" gets said about things nobody decided.
  example: |
    Every decision-log entry carries one of the three states plus evidence.

field approval
  prompt: Has the sponsor signed this off? Write who, and when.
  check date: A sign-off with no date is not a sign-off. Say who approved it and on what day.
  label: Charter sign-off
  type: line
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Output of 4.1 Develop Project Charter: the charter is approved by the
    sponsor.
  why: |
    The signature is what turns a draft into an authorized project.
  if_blank: |
    Planning work starts on something nobody authorized.
  example: Approved by the sponsor, 2026-09-15.
